wonderful time.

Vespertilion: It is my honor. But I must admit confusion. I do not believe
we've met? From where did you receive an invitation?

Chameleon: Alisa. Alisa Nonamus. And it was sent by a member of your staff, of
course. I'm sure you'll find me on the guest list.

Vespertilion: I see. I hope you will accept my sincerest apologies; it is wrong
of me to question an invited guest who has crossed my threshold.

[============================================================================]

The butler is picking up the curtain rod.

Chameleon: I've heard so much about the architecture of your mansion. It's
fascinating to finally see it in person!

Vespertilion: Yes, certainly. The first Count Vespertilion had his
certain...eccentricities. The architecture reflects them. And we have sought to
preserve that history through the current renovations. He, for instance,
refused to let any plumbing pass through the ceilings or floors, only through
walls, preferably external.

Chameleon: That must have been hard to maintain in the new Glass Wing. But the
flow of water isn't something you, personally, have strong feelings about?

Vespertilion: By no means. But the National Register of Historic Places does.

[============================================================================]

The butler is moving from the trophy room to the granite-gilded passage.

Vespertilion: I hope you will not think me rude, but I must take my leave.
There are many other guests to attend to. Please enjoy the hospitality of my
home, and should anything be lacking, the butler will be on hand within
moments.

Chameleon: Thank you, my lord. I already feel much enriched being here. I'm
sure the rest of the night will be just as profitable.

